The program runs the standard fannkuch-redux algorithm, splits the work into chunks and processes them in parallel.

**Permutation order.** Permutations are produced by the usual count-array scheme. Step `k` rotates the first `i+1` elements. `count[i]` tracks how many rotations have been applied at each level.

**Splitting the work.** The `n!` permutations are divided into contiguous index ranges, and each range is processed on its own thread. A range can start at any permutation index. To do that, the program decodes the start index into its permutation using the factorial number system. Each digit `d` of the index sets `count[i]` and rotates the prefix left by `d` places.

**Counting flips.** Flipping a permutation of length `f+1` sends the old element at position `f` to the front and puts the old front value at position `f`. The value at the front is always `f` itself. So each flip is done in three steps:
1. Read `t = q[f]`.
2. Set `q[f] = f`.
3. Reverse the inner segment `q[1..f-1]`.

The new front value is then `t`, and the loop repeats until the front is 0.

**Checksum.** Each permutation's flip count is added if its global index is even and subtracted if it is odd. Each thread works from absolute indices, so the parity is correct in every chunk. The per-thread maximum and checksum are then combined.

